  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThrough Saturday night: Dry and cooler conditions will prevail in\nthe wake of a cold front that pushed across the area last night \nthrough this morning. Though it remains seasonably mild today, we \nare seeing the influx of much drier air behind the front. Colder \nair will begin to arrive tonight, and sheltered areas in northeast\nMS could get close to freezing tomorrow morning. However, the \ncoldest mornings will come Saturday and Sunday mornings, with a \nfreeze possible across much of north and east MS and frost \npossible over a broader area. A Freeze Watch has been posted for \nmany of these areas for Friday night into Saturday, with a warning\nand additional products for Saturday night likely to follow. \n\nBecause few areas have received wetting rains recently, the \ncombination of low humidity and breezy conditions in the wake of \nthe front is resulting in elevated fire concerns today. We'll see \na similar situation tomorrow with even lower RH anticipated during\nthe day. Lower daytime temperatures may help mitigate the threat \nsomewhat, but given the ongoing drought/dry fuels and continued \nbreezy conditions, fire danger will persist.\n\nSunday through next Thursday: In the wake of the longwave upper\ntrough currently over the eastern CONUS, a secondary shortwave\nwill follow Sunday into Monday, with a surface system tracking \nfrom Texas into the northern Gulf. Scattered showers on the \nnorthern periphery of this system may spread into southern \nportions of our area late Sunday into Monday, though there are \nsome model solutions showing little to no rain making it into our \narea. Regardless, any rainfall with this system would be light. \nOtherwise, surface ridging will build back in through mid-week \nwith dry weather and gradually moderating temperatures. /DL/",
